<Layout title="Context maps and semantic bridges" description="How strategic context maps describe authority and collaboration, while term mappings, shared meanings and transformations connect models at a different level.">
  <h1>Context maps and semantic bridges</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    A context map explains how independently owned models work together: who supplies
    meaning, who consumes it, what agreement crosses the boundary, and where translation
    protects a local model. It is a map of responsibilities as well as connections.
  </p>

  <h2 id="two-scales">Map the relationship before mapping the terms</h2>
  <p>
    Imagine a conveyancing service consuming an address supplied by a property-data
    service. A term mapping can explain how one address concept corresponds to the
    other. It cannot explain who changes the exchange format, whether consumers can
    influence it, or what happens when the provider's interpretation changes.
    Those are context-map questions.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>Four related agreements at different levels</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Agreement</th><th scope="col">What it answers</th><th scope="col">What it does not establish</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Strategic context map</th><td>How do the owners collaborate, depend on each other and handle change?</td><td>That any two terms are equivalent.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Common-boundary contract</th><td>Which small meanings and conventions must consumers share?</td><td>That all local concepts belong in a universal model.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Term correspondence</th><td>How are these two versioned meanings related for this purpose?</td><td>That data can be transformed without loss.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Delivery transformation</th><td>How do particular records become the receiving representation?</td><td>That the source and target mean the same thing in every context.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="boundary">What makes a boundary real?</h2>
  <p>
    Look for a coherent working language and an authority able to maintain it. Two
    applications can implement the same context. One organisation can contain
    several contexts. A subject such as “property” can appear throughout them.
    Neither an organisation chart nor a database inventory settles the boundary.
  </p>
  <p>
    A useful boundary record names the model, its owners, the decisions they can make,
    representative questions, essential terms and exclusions. If a disagreement can
    be resolved by the same accountable community, it may be one model needing
    clarification. If two communities legitimately need different meanings and
    neither can impose one on the other, an explicit boundary and mapping may be
    the more honest design.
  </p>
  <p>
    RDF named graphs can help organise assertions, derived views and provenance, but
    putting data in a named graph does not by itself establish a bounded context or
    governance authority. A namespace is an identifier convention; a context is a
    meaning-and-responsibility agreement. See
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/bounded-contexts#homes">semantic homes</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="patterns">Choose an explicit collaboration pattern</h2>
  <p>
    Domain-driven design gives useful names to recurring relationships. These patterns
    describe choices to discuss with the participants; the examples below are
    illustrative, not assertions about agreements already signed by OPDA groups.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>Context relationship patterns and property-data questions</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Pattern</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th><th scope="col">Question to settle</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Customer / Supplier</th><td>An upstream supplier and downstream consumer explicitly negotiate needs and change.</td><td>Can conveyancing requirements influence the property-data exchange, and through which decision route?</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Partnership</th><td>Two owners coordinate delivery because success depends on both models evolving together.</td><td>Which changes must agency and conveyancing participants review jointly?</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Shared Kernel</th><td>A deliberately small part of the model is shared and changed under joint discipline.</td><td>What exactly is shared, and who must agree before it changes?</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Published Language</th><td>A documented exchange vocabulary provides an agreed way to communicate.</td><td>Which version defines the message, its terms and its allowed values?</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Open Host Service</th><td>A provider exposes a defined integration protocol for multiple consumers.</td><td>How do consumers discover and use the supported interface?</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Anti-Corruption Layer</th><td>A consumer translates an external model so its own meaning remains intact.</td><td>Which external statuses require interpretation before they enter a lending workflow?</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Conformist</th><td>A downstream consumer follows an upstream model without meaningful influence over it.</td><td>What dependencies and compromises are accepted, and when should translation be added?</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Separate Ways</th><td>The owners deliberately decide that integration is not justified for the scoped need.</td><td>Why is there no bridge, and what future need would reopen the decision?</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Several patterns can describe different aspects of one relationship: a supplier
    may publish a language while a consumer uses an anti-corruption layer. Record
    who plays each part. Symmetric collaboration is different from upstream/downstream
    dependence, so an unlabeled arrow is rarely enough. An absent arrow means
    “not recorded”; it does not prove a deliberate Separate Ways decision.
  </p>

  <h2 id="record">What a useful context-map record contains</h2>
  <p>
    Treat the connection as a reviewable record, not simply a line on a slide. Name
    the two contexts, the pattern, direction, source of authority, business purpose,
    responsible owners, exchange contract and change process. Identify whether the
    map describes today's arrangement or a proposed one. A desired relationship must
    not be presented as an operational dependency.
  </p>
  <p>
    Link the connection to the particular term correspondences that support it.
    Attaching every mapping in a large mapping set conceals which ones actually apply.
    Keep the version, decision and evidence for both the strategic agreement and its
    term-level records. A technical change may affect one without changing the other.
  </p>
  <p>
    The selected ODR-0127 profile exposes active AS-IS and TO-BE map projections;
    files and version control retain revision history. It does not attach a chain
    of <code>prov:wasDerivedFrom</code> or <code>prov:wasRevisionOf</code> statements
    directly to strategic relationships. Evidence for a specific exclusion or
    agreement and provenance for a term mapping remain separately scoped records.
  </p>

  <h2 id="worked-example">Worked example: an address used in a transaction</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Name the need.</strong> A conveyancer needs to locate a source record relevant to the property in an instruction.</li>
    <li><strong>Name the responsibilities.</strong> The source provider governs its address record; the conveyancing context governs the instruction and its interpretation.</li>
    <li><strong>Choose the relationship.</strong> For illustration, the provider publishes a language and the consumer translates it at its boundary.</li>
    <li><strong>Compare the meanings.</strong> Is the supplied address a postal delivery address, a site description or an address attached to a registry entry? What does the receiving term require?</li>
    <li><strong>Record the correspondence.</strong> Preserve both terms if their definitions differ. Explain the applicable direction and limitations instead of asserting identity from matching text.</li>
    <li><strong>Check the delivery.</strong> Confirm that the transformed record retains the source identifier, version and any distinctions needed by the receiving profile.</li>
    <li><strong>Review change.</strong> A new source definition or expanded consumer purpose can reopen the correspondence without forcing every other context to rename its terms.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="term-mappings">Then choose the term-level bridge</h2>
  <p>
    SKOS offers exact, close, broader, narrower and related concept mappings, each
    with different semantics. They are not interchangeable with individual identity
    or OWL class equivalence. A class-to-class or property-to-property mapping needs
    its own reviewed representation. Read the
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/evidence-and-mappings#skos-predicates">predicate guide</a>
    before selecting a relation.
  </p>
  <p>
    SSSOM captures a mapping's justification and provenance;
    it cannot make an incorrect relation correct. Its use in the reference modelling
    method is normative for our mapping discipline: source ODR-0087, ODR-0096 and
    ODR-0098 require SSSOM records and SEMAPV justification. The current candidate
    still has no emitted mappings; the
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/standards#standard-sssom">standards register</a>
    separates that implementation gap from the selected rule.
  </p>

  <h2 id="status">Method and implementation</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/modelling/adr/adr-0063">ADR-0063</a> and
    <a href="/modelling/adr/adr-0067">ADR-0067</a> establish domain-led ownership and
    the small common boundary. The normative source method develops strategic context maps
    further in ODR-0127. Today's
    <a href="/development/property-pack/contexts">Property Pack context views</a>
    show candidate homes; they are not evidence that the full strategic mapping
    model or its agreements are implemented. The
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/decision-basis">decision guide</a> records that boundary.
  </p>
</Layout>
